<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Great Sioux Nation</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1877 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Oceti Sakowin, or Seven Council Fires, of the Dakota, Nakota and Lakota peoples, who became the leading power of the northern Plains in the 19th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The seven divisions of the Oceti Sakowin, the four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakota-people">Dakota peoples</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yankton-sioux">Yankton</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanktonai">Yanktonai</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakota-people">Lakota</a> or Teton, originally lived around the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesota">Minnesota</a>. Pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> and drawn by the bison, many moved west in the 18th century, acquired horses, and by around 1776 the Lakota had reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The divisions were connected by kinship, language and ceremony rather than a single government; each band, or tiyospaye, had its own leaders, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakota-people">Lakota</a> were themselves divided into seven groups. Mounted bison hunting supplied food, hides and trade goods, and ceremonies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-dance">Sun Dance</a> renewed communal bonds. In the 1860s Red Cloud's War forced the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> to accept the Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868, creating a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-sioux-reservation">Great Sioux Reservation</a> that included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After gold was found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-hills">Black Hills</a> in 1874, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-sioux-war-of-1876">Great Sioux War</a> followed; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakota-people">Lakota</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheyenne">Cheyenne</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sitting-bull">Sitting Bull</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crazy-horse">Crazy Horse</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-armstrong-custer">Custer</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-little-bighorn">Little Bighorn</a> in 1876. In 1877 Congress seized the Black Hills, and the Lakota were confined to shrinking reservations.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/great-sioux-nation.jpg" alt="Great Sioux Nation" width="320" height="441"><figcaption>Image: David F. Barry, Photographer, Bismarck, Dakota Territory · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sitting_Bull_by_D_F_Barry_ca_1883_Dakota_Territory.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1877 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Red Cloud, Sitting Bull, Crazy Horse</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lakota and Dakota traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lakota, Dakota</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oceti Sakowin, Seven Council Fires, Lakota</dd></dl></aside></div>

